This confocal microscopy image (Fig. 4) illustrates the uptake of fluorescently labeled Nano-11 and ovalbumin (OVA) by bone marrow-derived dendritic cells (BMDCs). It shows that while Nano-11 (red) is efficiently internalized by BMDCs, soluble OVA (green) alone is poorly taken up. Crucially, when OVA is adsorbed onto Nano-11, both Nano-11 and OVA colocalize within the BMDCs, demonstrating that Nano-11 significantly enhances the intracellular delivery of the antigen. This efficient antigen uptake by dendritic cells is presented as a key mechanism contributing to Nano-11's adjuvant effect.